        <!-- ═══ KATAX-CORE ═══ -->

        <section id="core" class="doc-section">
          <div class="section-accent"></div>
          <h2><span class="section-icon">&#9679;</span> katax-core</h2>
          <p><strong>katax-core</strong> is the validation layer. The entry point is <code>k</code>. Supports 20+ schema types, coercion, preprocess, async validators, composition and error utilities.</p>

          <h3>Quick Start</h3>
          <pre><code>{`import { k, type kataxInfer } from 'katax-core';

const UserSchema = k.object({
  email: k.email(),
  name: k.string().minLength(2).maxLength(100),
  age: k.number().min(18).optional(),
});

type User = kataxInfer<typeof UserSchema>;

const result = UserSchema.safeParse(req.body);
if (!result.success) return res.status(400).json({ errors: result.issues });

// Inferred: { email: string; name: string; age: number | undefined }`}</code></pre>

          <h3>Core Types</h3>
          <pre><code>{`type SafeParseResult<T> = 
  | { success: true; data: T }
  | { success: false; issues: Issue[] }

interface Issue {
  path: (string | number)[];
  message: string;
}

interface ValidationResult {
  valid: boolean;
  issues: Issue[];
}`}</code></pre>

          <h3>Schema API</h3>
          <p>All schemas implement: <code>parse(input): T</code> &middot; <code>safeParse(input): SafeParseResult&lt;T&gt;</code> &middot; <code>validate(input): ValidationResult</code> &middot; <code>parseAsync(input): Promise&lt;T&gt;</code> &middot; <code>safeParseAsync(input): Promise&lt;AsyncSafeParseResult&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> &middot; <code>isValidAsync(input): Promise&lt;AsyncValidationResult&gt;</code> &middot; <code>hasAsyncValidation(): boolean</code> &middot; <code>describe(text)</code> &middot; <code>toJsonSchema()</code> &middot; <code>kataxInfer: T</code></p>

          <h3>All k factories</h3>
          <pre><code>{`k.string()    k.number()    k.boolean()    k.bigint()   k.date()    k.twoDates()
k.object()    k.array()     k.tuple()      k.record()   k.set()     k.map()
k.union()     k.intersection()             k.discriminatedUnion()   k.lazy()
k.email()     k.file()      k.base64()     k.promise()
k.custom()    k.literal()   k.enum()
k.any()       k.unknown()   k.never()
k.nan()       k.null()      k.undefined()  k.void()
k.coerce      k.preprocess()`}</code></pre>

          <h3>Modifiers (chainable, all schemas)</h3>
          <pre><code>{`schema.optional()             // T | undefined
schema.nullable()             // T | null
schema.nullish()              // T | null | undefined
schema.default(value)         // Returns default if undefined
schema.transform(fn)          // Transform output type
schema.catch(value)           // Return fallback on validation failure
schema.refine(fn, msg?)       // Extra sync check
schema.asyncRefine(fn)        // Extra async check (DB, APIs...)
schema.brand('UserId')        // Nominal type
schema.describe('text')       // Description for JSON Schema / OpenAPI`}</code></pre>

          <h3>optional vs nullable vs nullish</h3>
          <p><code>undefined</code> (missing key) and <code>null</code> (explicitly empty) are different values. <code>.optional()</code> does <strong>not</strong> accept <code>null</code>; use <code>.nullish()</code> for both.</p>
          <div class="table-wrapper">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th style="width:30%">Modifier</th><th>undefined / missing key</th><th>null</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>.optional()</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>.nullable()</code></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>.nullish()</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>.default(v)</code></td><td>Yes (replaced by <code>v</code>)</td><td>No</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>

